          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %strong Löwenheim-Skolem theorem
            is a basic result in the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+theory"} model theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order logic
            and is part of a family of closely related theorems that concern the relation between structures or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models"} models
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+theories"} first-order theories
            of different
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
          %p
            One idea behind it is that finitary first-order logic by itself cannot pin down the cardinality of infinite structures or models: roughly speaking, Löwenheim-Skolem says that given such a structure
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            of different cardinalities. This gives an elementary reason why one cannot expect absolute
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categoricity"} categoricity
            for first-order theories; after this theorem was introduced, attention shifted to the question of categoricity in powers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            (i.e., how many models there are of a given cardinality
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stability+theory"} stability theory
          %p
            Historically, the theorem was one of the first results in model theory with papers published by Leopold Löwenheim (1915) and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thoralf+Skolem"} Thoralf Skolem
            (1920) which helped establishing first-order logic in the center of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+logic"} mathematical logic
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paradox"} paradox
            %em countable
            model of ZFC, with the paradoxical consequence that (externally speaking) the model admits no uncountable sets but yet (internally speaking) it has them. As usually happens with “paradoxes” (cf.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach-Tarski+paradox"} Banach-Tarski paradox
          %p
            Although the theorem fails in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second-order+logic"} second-order logic
            (for example, note one has absolute categoricity for the concept of complete ordered field), variants hold in infinitary logic with countably infinite disjunctions and in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sketches"} sketches
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abstract+model+theory"} abstract model theory
            e.g. in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lindstr%C3%B6m%27s+theorems"} Lindström's theorems
            on the characterization of standard
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order logic
            %sup#fnref:1
              %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
          %h2#statements_and_proofs Statements and proofs
          %p
            One frequently sees the Löwenheim-Skolem theorem separated into an “upward” version (which produces
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+embedding"} elementary extensions
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactness+theorem"} compactness theorem
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            be a first-order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signature"} signature
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            be a set of sentences of cardinality
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            has a model of cardinality
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                    %mo ≥
                    %msub
                      %mi ℵ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa \geq \aleph_{0}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ≥
                    %mi max
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi ℵ
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi β
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda \geq \max(\aleph_{0}, \beta)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            has a model of cardinality
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            has a model of infinite cardinality, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            has a model of cardinality
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℵ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \aleph_{0}
          %h2#with_paraconsistent_logic With paraconsistent logic
          %p
            According to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Bremer"} Bremer
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paraconsistent+logic"} paraconsistent
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+logic"} first order logic
          %blockquote
            %p Any mathematical theory presented in first order logic has a finite paraconsistent model.
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Skolem%27s+paradox"} Skolem's paradox
            %li
